Handling Uncalled Functions and Tasks in Partition Compile
Handling Uncalled Functions and Tasks in Partition Compile
When a design is compiled using the partition compile flow, all functions and tasks that
are compiled are considered for coverage computation. However, the inclusion of uncalled
functions and tasks in coverage calculation gives artificially low coverage scores. Single
compile already excludes uncalled functions and tasks from coverage monitoring. To do
the same in the partition compile flow, uncalled functions and tasks can be excluded from
coverage computation using the -cm_instr prune_uncalled_tf switch at compile time.
The -cm_instr prune_uncalled_tf switch also allows you to merge the coverage
databases generated using the single-compile flow with the coverage databases
generated in the partition-compile flow if a design contains uncalled functions or tasks.
The coverage databases generated using -cm_instr prune_uncalled_tf
in the Partition Compile flow are not merge-compatible with the coverage
databases generated without the switch in the Partition Compile flow if the
design contains uncalled functions or tasks.
For more information, see the following sections:
Consider the following example that shows two functions, myFn and myFn2 in the top
module:
function myFn(input reg arg);
automatic reg a,b,c;
if ( arg == 1'b0 )
myFn = a;
myFn = b;
endfunction;
function myFn2(input reg arg);
automatic reg a,b,c;
if ( (arg && a) || b )
myFn2 = a;
myFn2 = b;
endfunction;

Interface Expansion Limitations
The following limitations apply for interface expansion at the port boundary using the
-cm_tgl fullintf option:
• Enums are not supported within interfaces. You can infer real values of enum changes
from FSM coverage, which shows transitions between two enum states. For example:
interface intf(input bit clk);
typedef enum reg[1:0] {pink,red,yellow} color;
color d;
clocking master_cb @(posedge clk);
inout d;
endclocking
modport master (import master_cb.d);
endinterface

module tb();
logic clock;
intf intf_0(clock);
• Port interfaces which have an instance array in the path between the ref and actual
handle are not supported. Consider the following example:
interface wbone;
logic cyc_o;
modport dut_slave( output cyc_o);
endinterface

tb::cls::cg
Edge Biasing to Improve Coverage of Corner Values
The edge values of intervals can have direct mapping to the coverage bins. Biasing these
bins to cover the corner (edge) values helps to improve the overall coverage.
In the following example, the probability to cover the ALL_ZERO and ALL_HIGH bins is very
low. To improve the hit probability, biasing is enhanced to cover the edge values. This
feature helps to improve the overall URG score, although it can degrade the diversity
scores marginally.
Example 4 Testcase, test.v
covergroup rand_var_cg with function sample(bit[31:0] rand_var);
option.per_instance = 1;
cp_rand_var: coverpoint rand_var{
bins ALL_ZERO = {0};
bins MIDDLE[10] = {[32'h1:32'hffff_fffe]};
bins ALL_HIGH = {32'hffff_ffff};
}

class Bus;
rand bit[31:0] addr;
constraint cstr {
addr inside { [32'h0:32'hFFFF_FFFF]};

}

rand_var_cg cg = new();
Bus b = new();

repeat(100) begin
b.randomize();
cg.sample(b.addr);
end
end
To enable the edge biasing, you must add +32768 to the
+ntb_solver_bias_mode=<value> option.
Running Testcase Without Edge Biasing Enabled
Run the testcase, test.v, without edge biasing as follows:
% simv +ntb_solver_bias_mode=24841 +ntb_solver_mode=2

% urg -dir simv.vdb -report urg_rpt_24841 -format both
