Example: Coverpoint on a Real Expression
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp: coverpoint my_real_signal {
bins low = {[0.0:1.0]};
bins mid = {[1.0:2.0]};
bins high = {[2.0:3.0]};
}
In this example, the coverpoint cp is defined on the real-valued signal my_real_signal,
and explicit bins are provided to cover different ranges.
When you define a coverpoint on a real expression, you must explicitly specify at least one
user-defined bin. This is because bins are not automatically created for coverpoints of real
expressions.
If you do not specify at least one explicit user bin for a real-valued coverpoint, the following
compile error is issued:
Error-[FCNOBNRCP] No User Bin in Real Valued Coverpoint
test.sv, 4
top, "top::cg"
User bin is not present in coverpoint cp of covergroup top::cg.
Coverpoint of real expression must specify at least one explicit bins
construct.
This error is also issued if only illegal or ignore bins are present in a real-valued
coverpoint.
Bins of Real-Valued Coverpoint
User-Defined Bins
You can explicitly specify the ranges or values for bins. This is useful when you want to
track coverage for specific intervals or values.
covergroup temp_cg @(posedge clk);
temp_cp: coverpoint temperature {
bins freezing = {[ -40.0 : 0.0 ]};
bins cold = {[ 0.0 : 10.0 ]};
bins warm = {[ 10.0 : 25.0 ]};
bins hot = {[ 25.0 : 50.0 ]};
}
In this example, the coverpoint temp_cp is defined on the real-valued signal temperature,
with bins covering different temperature ranges: freezing, cold, warm, and hot.
Sample URG Report

Bin Name Range Covered Percent
low [0.0:1.0] Yes 100%
mid [1.0:2.0] No 0%
high [2.0:3.0] Yes 100%
Auto Bins
Auto bins partition a range into intervals automatically, based on the type option for real
intervals.
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp: coverpoint real_val {
bins auto[] = {[0.0:3.0]};
}
If type_option.real_interval is set to 1.0, this creates three bins: [0.0:1.0),
[1.0:2.0), [2.0:3.0].
Sample URG Report
Bin Name Range Covered Percent
auto[0] [0.0:1.0) Yes 100%
auto[1] [1.0:2.0) No 0%
auto[2] [2.0:3.0] Yes 100%
Cross Coverage
You can cross two coverpoints to see which combinations of bins are hit.
covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
cp1: coverpoint real_val1 {
bins a[] = {[0.0:2.0]};
}
cp2: coverpoint real_val2 {
bins b[] = {[1.0:3.0]};
}
cross_cp: cross cp1, cp2;

