Chapter 14: Gate-Level Simulation
Using Module Path Delay Feature to Reduce Pulses
Using Module Path Delay Feature to Reduce Pulses
VCS supports two delay modes. These are transport delay mode and inertial delay mode.
The transport delay mode allows all pulses that are narrower than the delay. Inertial delay
mode filters out all pulses that are narrower than the delay.
The delay simulation model has a gate level simulation that accurately models glitch
propagation through multiple logic levels. This model can simulate glitch decaying
behavior by using the following parameters:
• decay_rate
The percentage (rate) at which the pulse gets reduced. For example, if the decay rate
is 30, then 30% of the glitch duration decays. The remaining 70% propagates through.
• min_pulse_percent
Percentage (%) value of the module path delay. It can be an integer in the range of 0 to
100.
• min_pulse_const
Constant integer number in the range of 0 to 65535.
This model internally enables the transport delay mode.
Use the following options at compile-time:
% vcs -decay_rate=<x> -min_pulse_percent=<y> -min_pulse_const=<z time
unit>
x is an integer percentage between 0 to 100.
y is an integer percentage between 0 to 100.
z is an integer between 0 to 65536. Time unit can be ns, ps, fs and so on.
If you are using a configuration file, use the following compile time option:
% vcs +optconfigfile+<CONFIG_FILE_NAME> -decay_rate=0
The configuration file contains a list of modules that need decay/pulse shortening. The
following are a few examples of the modules in the configuration file:
module {module1} {decay_rate = 50, min_pulse_percent =
20, min_pulse _const = "1ns"}

Chapter 14: Gate-Level Simulation
Using Module Path Delay Feature to Reduce Pulses
module {module2} {decay_rate = 40, min_pulse_percent =
23, min_pulse _const = "1fs"}
The previous behavior remains unchanged for a regular pulse, that is, pulses
larger than module path delay. Only short (glitch) pulses are subject to the
reduction/ decay from this new feature.
