Compiling SystemC Designs in Partition Compile Flow
SystemC Partition Compile Example
The code shown in Example 1 is first explained in terms of compiling the SystemC design
and then in terms of how to partition your design and then compile.
Consider a code snippet (Example 1) that contains a top-level module named top which is
the OSCI name provided as an argument to sc_top.
Example 1 System C Partition Compile Example Code
int sc_main(int argc, char **argv)
{
static int init_done = 0;
sc_top *top;

if (!init_done) {
top = new sc_top("top");
init_done = 1;
}
sc_start(40, SC_NS);
}
To compile the code shown in Example 1, use the following command:
% vcs -partcomp -timescale=1ns/1ps -sysc top -o simv2
Partitioning the Design
To partition the design shown in Example 1, use a partition configuration file. Write the
configuration file as shown in Example 2 which shows a partition configuration file for
Example 1.
Example 2 SystemC Partition Compile Configuration File
//partcomp config file partcomp_cfg.v
config partcomp_cfg;
design VCS_SYSC_LIB.sc_main;
partition instance sc_main.sc_top.vlog_mod_o use
DEFAULT.vlog_mod;
default liblist DEFAULT;
endconfig
In Example 2, partcomp_cfg.v is the name of the configuration file. The configuration file
can be any .v file. The configuration file must start with config<config_modulename>;
and end with endconfig. The module name in Example 2 is partcomp_cfg.

